1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,401
Starting with a single atom, explore the limits of semiconductor device performance.

2
00:00:06,142 --> 00:00:12,339
AtomTrans—a quantum transport simulation platform built on plane-wave calculations and Wannier-basis Hamiltonians.

3
00:00:13,187 --> 00:00:19,539
Starting from plane-wave first-principles calculations, AtomTrans uses Wannierization to construct localized Hamiltonians

4
00:00:19,564 --> 00:00:24,892
and the nonequilibrium Green’s function method to connect atomic-scale electronic structure with device transport.

5
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:32,556
From molecular junctions and nanowires to two-dimensional semiconductors, build devices in an intuitive three-dimensional workspace.

6
00:00:33,297 --> 00:00:38,971
Define electrodes, channels, gates, and dielectric regions, bringing materials and device design together.

7
00:00:39,787 --> 00:00:45,847
For transistor research, investigate how gate voltage controls the channel and how current responds to source–drain bias.

8
00:00:46,588 --> 00:00:50,753
Examine transmission spectra, local densities of states, and transport pathways

9
00:00:50,778 --> 00:00:54,571
to understand contact effects, quantum tunneling, and switching behavior.

10
00:00:55,387 --> 00:01:00,204
Explore spin-dependent transport, superconductivity, phonons, and heat transport.

11
00:01:00,957 --> 00:01:05,463
Extend your research with semiclassical transport and machine-learned Hamiltonian workflows.

12
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:13,213
From structure modeling and task monitoring to visualization and result comparison, work within one integrated scientific environment.

13
00:01:14,053 --> 00:01:17,591
AtomTrans. Connecting electronic structure with device transport.
